<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Managed App Configuration (first-install verification)

  Applies to both identities — AutoSignDisplay (edu.princeton.autosigndisplay) and
  AutoStreamDisplay (edu.princeton.autostreamdisplay). App Configuration is scoped to
  an app in Jamf, so this payload carries no bundle identifier and is valid for either.

  Purpose: prove that managed configuration is reaching the app on a real
  MDM-managed Apple TV, before locking anything down. Deliberately NOT a kiosk
  payload — SettingsDisabled, ViewOnlyMode and SettingsPIN are all absent so you can
  walk the UI and confirm what landed.

  How to read the result from across the room, with no logs and no console:

    - The heading says "MDM VERIFY — <location>". If instead it shows the app's own
      name — "AutoSignDisplay" or "AutoStreamDisplay", whichever you installed, since
      the fallback heading is CFBundleDisplayName — the payload did not arrive.
    - The Stream Presets list shows exactly two entries, "Verify Scenic" and
      "Verify Announcements", rather than the built-in defaults. Those differ by
      identity: the private build ships four Princeton channels (News, News Plus,
      Scenic, Announcements), the public build ships one, "Featured Event Stream".
    - The stream starts on its own, because PlayOnAppOpen is true.
    - Settings > Recovery shows Retry Timeout as 7s. That value is not one of the
      six the on-device UI cycles through (3, 5, 10, 15, 30, 60), so seeing it proves
      the number came from the payload rather than from a local default.

  The stream URLs are Princeton's own live channels, which redirect to Kaltura HLS
  manifests and need no credentials.

  Then apply jamf-app-config-kiosk.xml to test the locked-down configuration.

  CHANGING THIS PAYLOAD — do not force-quit first. From 1.1 configuration is
  declarative: the app reconciles while running, so editing the payload in Jamf and
  waiting is itself the test. Expect the change within roughly RetryTimeout + 1.5s of
  it landing on the device — about 9s with the 7s below — on top of however long Jamf
  takes to push.

  Force-quitting or rebooting also picks the change up, but it proves only that launch
  reads the payload, which 1.0 already did. Reach for it as a fallback after a reconcile
  has failed to happen, never as the first move — it destroys the evidence.

  One precondition: the poll rides the playback watchdog's tick, which runs whenever a
  player exists and the app is foregrounded — including while sitting on the channel
  list. It does not run after someone has explicitly pressed Stop. Verify reconcile from
  a playing or auto-playing state, which is also the only state a kiosk is ever in.

  See "Applying a changed payload" in README.md.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- Name the actual display. This is the canary: visible instantly, and unique
       enough that it cannot be confused with the app's own default heading. -->
  <key>DisplayTitle</key>
  <string>MDM VERIFY — Sherrerd Hall</string>

  <key>PlayOnAppOpen</key>
  <true/>

  <key>AutoResume</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- 7 is deliberately not one of the values the Settings screen offers (3, 5, 10,
       15, 30, 60). If the UI displays 7s, the payload is being honored. -->
  <key>RetryTimeout</key>
  <real>7.0</real>

  <key>ChannelPresets</key>
  <array>
    <dict>
      <key>Name</key>
      <string>Verify Scenic</string>
      <key>URL</key>
      <string>https://orfe.princeton.edu/live/scenic</string>
    </dict>
    <dict>
      <key>Name</key>
      <string>Verify Announcements</string>
      <key>URL</key>
      <string>https://orfe.princeton.edu/live/announcements</string>
    </dict>
  </array>

  <key>DefaultChannel</key>
  <string>https://orfe.princeton.edu/live/scenic</string>
</dict>
</plist>
